## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to translate edge ML costs into a robust business case, including privacy constraints, rollout risk, and financial metrics.

## Key Concepts
- On-device RL inference energy and latency
- Policy delta encoding and secure transmission
- Drift/maintenance costs and OTA rollout
- Privacy/compliance and data minimization
- Rollout strategy and go/no-go criteria

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-cost estimator
function estimateCost(params) {
  const {
    dispatchesPerHour, energyPerDispatch, latencyMs,
    cloudSavingsPerDispatch
  } = params;
  // simplified calculation
  return energyPerDispatch * dispatchesPerHour / 3600;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate model drift in production and trigger rollback?
- What would you put in the go/no-go criteria to scale beyond pilot?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about client-side privacy, offline UX, and cost trade-offs without server trust. The candidate must specify concrete numbers, crypto choices, and go/no-go criteria, balancing performance, storage, and regulatory costs.

## Key Concepts

- Client-side encryption and key management
- Data growth and local cache sizing
- Delta synchronization vs full sync
- Privacy/compliance implications and auditability

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example using Web Crypto API AES-GCM
async function encrypt(plaintext, cryptoKey) {
  const iv = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(12));
  const encrypted = await crypto.subtle.encrypt(
    { name: 'AES-GCM', iv },
    cryptoKey,
    new TextEncoder().encode(plaintext)
  );
  return { encrypted, iv };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to translate a privacy-centric on-device feature into a full cost-benefit plan with rollout, compliance, and measurable business impact.

## Key Concepts
- On-device ML inference cost and model residency
- OTA update strategies and cadence
- Security/compliance for client-only processing
- Telemetry budget, data minimization, and privacy risk

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple per-device cost model snippet (illustrative)
function costPerDevice(modelSizeMB, energyW, updatesPerMonth){
  const energyCost = energyW * 0.01;
  const updateCost = updatesPerMonth * 0.5;
  return modelSizeMB * 0.01 + energyCost + updateCost;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate that the on-device model does not leak sensitive information?
- What calibration data would you collect to ensure break-even remains realistic across regions?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical on-device crypto trade-offs for privacy-focused apps.

## Key Concepts
- On-device key management
- Key rotation cadence
- AES-256-GCM encryption
- Impact on battery, CPU, memory
- Privacy/compliance costs

## Code Example
```javascript
async function encryptNote(note, key) {
  const enc = new TextEncoder();
  const iv = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(12));
  const alg = { name: 'AES-GCM', iv };
  const ciphertext = await crypto.subtle.encrypt(alg, key, enc.encode(note));
  return { iv, ciphertext: new Uint8Array(ciphertext) };
}
```

